# Shell functions currently over the size limit, frozen at their present length.
#
# Format: <path> | <function> | <lines> | <reason>
#
# The gate refuses growth, new offenders, AND a stale entry — shrinking a function
# means updating its number here, deleting it once it drops under the limit. That
# is what stops the baseline becoming cover for the next one.
#
# Length is weak evidence on its own; this list is a queue, not an accusation.
# The refactor entries live in docs/refactoring-backlog.md F1/F2.
docs/scripts/bump_versions.py | main | 159 | F1 2026-09-04 reviewed, blocked on coverage not shape: 137 code lines are an 8-flag argparse block plus the check/write/write-all dispatch. Outside the build closure and cuttable today, but nothing in linux/scripts/tests or docs/scripts covers it and it WRITES versions.env and checksums, so the prerequisite is a suite and then the split
docs/scripts/generate_sbom.py | build_document | 88 | F1 2026-09-04 reviewed, seam named: 80 code lines building SPDX packages and relationships from one triple-nested walk over the deps metadata. The seam is per-entry, _sbom_package(entry, versions), leaving build_document as the walk. Low risk and compare_sbom.py exists, but no in-repo suite asserts the document shape, so pair the split with one
linux/scripts/01-core/base-image.sh | install_nodejs | 81 | F1 2026-09-04 reviewed, KEEP at +1 over: a per-arch case whose riscv64 arm is a different mechanism entirely (ubuntu-ports packages plus the NODE_RISCV64_MAJOR_REQUIRED lag policy, returning before the tarball path) while amd64/arm64 share the download-verify-symlink tail. Seam if it grows: _install_nodejs_from_ports, ~30 lines
linux/scripts/01-core/cross-apt.sh | install_target_packages | 81 | F1 2026-09-04 reviewed, KEEP: a three-step escalation on ONE apt transaction (atomic install, per-package retry, dpkg-status arbitration) where each step reads the previous rc. Deciding 'foreign-arch postinst noise' vs 'one renamed name took the whole batch down' only works in one place; 25 of the 81 lines are the record of the callers whose tolerant `or-true` suffix silently lost ~20 libs
linux/scripts/01-core/cross-meson.sh | ensure_meson_cross_file | 93 | F1 2026-09-04 reviewed, KEEP: 56 lines of shell computing substitutions plus a 37-line `cat > <ini>` heredoc that IS the Meson cross file (6 sections). The emitted half is a document, not a function; splitting the computation off leaves a wrapper around a template
linux/scripts/01-core/cross-stage-build.sh | _cross_stage_build_impl | 91 | F1 reviewed, KEEP: the one place a stage build is assembled and run, so its 11 decisions are the stage contract itself -- cache flags, output/tag form, retry and salvage. Splitting further would put the retry on one side of a seam and the thing retried on the other. HISTORY: 170 -> 91 on 2026-09-03, four seams extracted
linux/scripts/01-core/parallel-loop.sh | run_parallel_arch_loop | 92 | F1 2026-09-04 reviewed, KEEP: the flagdir lifetime (mktemp, export PARALLEL_LOOP_FLAGDIR, harvest, rm) must stay one function with ONE exit point. Its own 12-line comment records that a RETURN trap for that cleanup fired on the CALLER's return and killed a green chain with '_flagdir: unbound variable'; splitting the parallel and sequential arms recreates the multi-exit shape the bug came from
linux/scripts/01-core/python_uv.sh | uv_sync_project | 109 | F1 2026-09-04 reviewed, SPLIT WANTED, blocked on coverage not shape: 72 code lines, and the extras selection (UV_SYNC_EXTRAS vs --all-extras plus _uv_extras_to_exclude, ~22 lines) is a clean seam -> _uv_sync_extra_args. Nothing in linux/scripts/tests exercises uv_sync_project, so the split would be unverifiable, and the other half is the venv pin that stopped `uv sync` writing into root-owned /opt/venv
