# --- EX1 2026-09-07: linux/llm-stack enters the scan set. These 47 rows are the
# territory the extent gates could never see; every one was read and given a
# verdict in the same wave that widened SCAN, so none is a bare baseline. 34 of
# them say DEBT and name their seam -- that is the honest state of a benchmark
# harness nobody had reviewed for shape. docs/refactoring-backlog.md EX1
linux/llm-stack/bench_agent.py | main | 106 | 106 lines = a 20-line argparse table (ten add_argument calls, 981-1000), three early-exit modes (--list prints the task table and returns, --self-test exits on self_test()'s bool, missing OPENCODE binary exits), 16 lines of task selection and missing-tool announcement (1013-1028), 5 lines of opencode provenance/env setup (1031-1035), the run comprehension with its keep-or-rmtree finally (1038-1045), 15 lines of score printing with the blocked and skipped caveats (1047-1061), and 23 lines assembling the write_report extra dict (1063-1085). Two clean seams: _select_tasks(args) -> (tasks, skipped) for 1013-1028, which owns the D18 contract that a --task typo or an all-unbuildable selection raises SystemExit instead of printing 0/0, plus the per-task 'SKIPPED ... NOT graded on this host' line; and _write_agent_report(...) for 1063-1085. Together they cut 39 lines to 67, under the 80 limit. Nothing blocks either: run_main in tests/test_bench_agent.py drives main end-to-end over both blocks (test_the_report_records_which_fixtures_were_skipped, test_selecting_only_an_unbuildable_task_exits_nonzero, TestReportProvenance). The only friction is that the report helper needs config, config_sha, config_path, note and base_url, which argues for collapsing 1031-1033 into one provenance value rather than a five-parameter signature.
linux/llm-stack/bench_coding.py | ask | 83 | 83 lines of which 25 are the docstring and 7 more are comment: 49 lines of code, comfortably under the 80 limit, in a function that is 3 over it because of prose that is load-bearing. The docstring is the only place in the repo that records that urlopen's `timeout` is PER SOCKET READ rather than per request, that a 4B model measured 2026-09-05 spent over an hour emitting deltas under an 8000-token budget and blocked the whole sweep, and that this is why the `deadline` parameter exists and why `gave_up` is returned as a distinct flag instead of an empty answer. The code under it is one streamed request: build the body with stream_options.include_usage, then one arm per field the two SSE dialects may carry, then return the nine-tuple. Delete or relocate the docstring and this row disappears without a line of logic moving, so the number measures documentation density, not shape -- if the repo's near-zero-comment convention is applied here the fix is a docs/*.md anchor, which is a comment-policy decision and not a refactor. The one real redundancy is the two-line `if ttft is None: ttft = time.monotonic() - started` guard written twice, once for a reasoning delta and once for a content delta; the two arms append to different lists, so merging them costs more structure than the two lines are worth.
linux/llm-stack/bench_coding.py | evaluate | 227 | 227 raw lines = 13 docstring + 41 comment + 6 blank + 167 code, and it is three functions in one body: 1616-1704 is ONE ATTEMPT (ask, extract_code, run_candidate, the skipped/gave_up/truncated cascade, the printed column line and the 20-key row dict), 1706-1787 is the aggregation and summary (nine `sum(1 for r in results if r.get(...))` counters, the per-task output-sha256 determinism test, effective_n/effective_k, the `extra` string assembly and four NOTE prints), and 1788-1807 is the report dict. The tell that this is already understood as two pieces is the indentation: `for task in TASKS:` at 1614 is followed by `for attempt in range(repeats):` indented TWO spaces at 1615 so the body stays at column 8 -- the body was written not to be re-indented, which is what an extraction the author did in their head looks like. Seam: _attempt_row(task, attempt, ...) -> row, and _summarise(results, repeats) -> the counter dict that both the prints and the return read. Each half is over the 80-line limit on its own, so this is a split into three, not a trim. Nothing blocks it: 17 tests call bc.evaluate directly and 13 mutations.json rows target it, so a behaviour-preserving cut is provable without a build.
linux/llm-stack/bench_coding.py | main | 181 | 181 lines = 157 code + 14 comment + 10 blank, no docstring, and it is a CLI table plus a report renderer that never belonged together: 1870-1919 is a pure argparse block (13 add_argument calls, ZERO branch points -- the complexity walker attributes none of main's 30 to it), 1921-1977 is the actual driver (the five-arm --task-set dispatch, grader_selfcheck and its skip lines, candidate_rows, the evaluate comprehension, mark_suspect_cases, write_report), and 1978-2049 is 72 lines of ranking presentation -- the is_control partition, the tiered table, the per-lang/per-kind matrix and the skipped/cut footnotes. Seam: print_ranking(reports, suspect), taking only those two values; the comment already at 1975 states the invariant that makes it safe ('Ranking last: it only prints, and a print must never be able to destroy a completed measurement'). One coupling survives that split and is a separate second cut: the task-set arm at 1921-1929 rebinds the MODULE GLOBAL TASKS which evaluate then reads, which is why tests have to monkeypatch bc.TASKS (test_bench_review_fixes.py:195) and save/restore it around main (test_bench_coding_audit.py:324) -- passing the task list into evaluate as a parameter would remove that. Six mutations.json rows target main, so both cuts are provable.
linux/llm-stack/bench_compare.py | compare | 182 | Real debt with a clean seam: 142 of the 182 lines are the per-label loop at line 227, and inside it four INDEPENDENT verdict families each append to the same `findings` list and OR into the same `regressed` flag — the per-case flip diff (231-281: strict/single_draw, _broke/_fixed/degraded), the aggregate score (283-331: effective_k fallback, diff_interval, paired_sign_test vs intervals_overlap), per-attempt timing (335-349: _per_attempt plus the legacy-source note), and the bench_lanes throughput/serialised pair (352-367); none of them reads the others' output. The seam is four helpers `_case_findings/_score_findings/_timing_findings/_throughput_findings(label, a, b, ctx) -> (lines, regressed)` with the preamble (benchmark mismatch, compare_provenance notes, the config-diff loop, the suspect banner, the missing/new label loops) staying in compare(); the only cross-section coupling is that `shared` and `strict`, computed by the case section, are consumed by the score section (`if shared:` picks paired over unpaired, `if degraded and not strict` gates the sampling-lane line), so the case helper must return them or a small per-label context must carry them. Nothing blocks the cut today: tests/test_bench_compare.py, test_bench_tools_evaluate.py and test_bench_coding_audit.py all drive compare() at its public boundary and assert on the finding strings, so the four helpers can be extracted behaviour-for-behaviour and the closures _rate/_broke/_fixed (currently redefined on every loop iteration) become module-level functions in the process.
linux/llm-stack/bench_compare.py | normalise | 92 | Two disjoint envelope adapters in one body, joined only by `if 'reports' in report` at line 65: the shared-envelope branch is 57 lines (65-121) and the benchmark_openai_api legacy branch is 21 (123-143), and they share nothing but the ValueError guard above them. Inside the shared branch, 49 of those lines are one per-report row builder — the inner scan over `r['results']` that drops rows failing `measured()` and accumulates `cases`/`walls`, the duplicate-label refusal (`any(e['label'] == label ...)`, which is a real refusal: two candidates resolving to one name make their scores unattributable), the determinism_probe inheritance from provenance when `r.get('base_url')` matches, and a 16-key entry literal that is a TABLE of field copies with six comment blocks explaining which producer writes which key. The seam is `_entry_from_report(r, prov)` for 68-116 plus `_normalise_legacy(report)` for 123-143, leaving a ~12-line dispatcher; the row builder is the piece worth its own name because it is where report-shape policy lives (which rows count, which label wins, where the probe comes from) and it is currently unreachable except through the whole adapter. Nothing blocks it: tests/test_bench_compare.py calls normalise() with both envelopes (line 34 shared, line 49 the legacy `{model, results:[{latency_s}]}` shape) and test_bench_coding_audit.py feeds it repeat-bearing reports, so both branches are already pinned from outside.
linux/llm-stack/bench_sweep.py | sweep | 97 | sweep is not one job but five sequential phases that all append into the single `summary` dict which becomes _sweep.json: plan() plus the summary literal (142-151), the per-candidate correctness-gate loop that fills `gates` and summary["candidates"] (153-174), the per-step loop that skips lanes whose gate said unreachable and records argv/returncode/written (176-199), the bench_report.py manifest call (201-212), the optional per-report bench_compare pass (214-223), and the tmp+os.replace write (225-229). The phases never interleave — they talk only through `gates`, `steps` and `written` — so flattening them protects no contract; the ordering that genuinely IS a contract (gate before measure, manifest after the steps, compare only over `written`) reads more plainly as four named calls in sequence than as 97 lines of straight-line body, which is the opposite of a precedence ladder. Seam: extract gate_all(candidates, args) -> (gates, candidate_rows), run_steps(steps, gates, args) -> step_rows, and a report tail (manifest_step + compare_written), each RETURNING its rows instead of mutating `summary` — otherwise the split only hides the coupling — leaving sweep at roughly twenty lines of plan/call/call/call/write. Nothing blocks that today: tests/test_bench_sweep.py drives sweep() about fifteen times with run_step and gate monkeypatched and already pins the skipped-gate row, the stored argv, the leading-underscore _sweep.json name and the `regressed` flag, so the extraction is verifiable without ever running a benchmark tool.
linux/llm-stack/bench_tools.py | evaluate | 161 | one function that runs two different suites and then reports on them: 827-839 tool-set resolution and the warmup, 842-853 the record/report_error closures over `results`, 855-884 the single-turn CASES loop (opencode translate/skip, repeats x variants, context padding, per-case print), 886-908 the multi-turn MULTI_CASES loop (kind dispatch to grade_followup vs grade_error_recovery), 910-941 the errored/measured partition plus determinism from message_sha256 and the per-category tally, 942-963 the printed summary, 964-981 an 18-key return dict. The seam is four named pieces -- _run_single_cases (855-884), _run_multi_cases (886-908), _summary_stats (910-941) and _print_summary (942-963), leaving evaluate as warmup plus four calls -- and it is measured, not hoped for: by the gate's own py_metrics those land at cc 14, 12, 15 and 9 with evaluate at 4, so the split clears both limits everywhere instead of relocating the offender. Nothing blocks it: linux/llm-stack is outside the build closure, and tests/test_bench_tools_evaluate.py already drives evaluate() end to end over stubbed call/call_multi with monkeypatched CASES/MULTI_CASES, asserting precisely the boundary a split could break (total, errored, effective_n, effective_k, deterministic, and the row shape bench_compare consumes). The real cost is bookkeeping: five docs/scripts/mutations.json entries (tools.multi-transport-scored-as-wrong, tools.errored-attempts-do-not-vote, tools.determinism-from-the-message-hash, tools.effective-k-count-not-ratio, tools.config-records-the-scoring-flags) anchor on literal source lines inside 855-981 including their leading whitespace, so re-indenting those lines into helpers breaks their `find` strings and they must be re-anchored in the same commit. The body is already signalling the pressure: line 864 steps the variant loop in by 2 spaces instead of 4 to keep the request call on the page.
linux/llm-stack/bench_tools.py | main | 141 | an arg parser plus a mode dispatcher with a ranking table bolted onto the end. 1008-1049 is 14 add_argument calls of which roughly 40 lines are help text, and that text is content rather than padding -- the --tools, --accept-text-json, --context-tokens and --prompt-variants help is where the reason each knob exists is written down; 1051-1104 is the deferred imports, candidate_rows, the --system read, the --turn-growth early return and the --output write_report. The second subject is 1109-1147, and it is not merely separable but already duplicated: it is a near line-for-line clone of bench_coding.py:1978-2020 -- same `contenders = [r for r in reports if not is_control(r)] or reports`, same "calibration only, not ranked" line, same sort key (-passed/total, -total, total_wall_s), same tiers(ranked, case_outcomes) loop with the tier rule and the "rows within one tier are NOT separated by the paired sign test" footer, differing only in the header, the label width (40 vs 34) and the row's columns. It belongs in bench_cli.py, whose own docstring calls it "the front end bench_coding.py and bench_tools.py share", as print_ranking(reports, suspect, header, row_fn). Nothing blocks it: the block only prints, which the code states at 1106-1107 and depends on, and llm-stack needs no chain. Honest limit of the cut: main lands at ~102 lines, so this row shrinks and gets re-baselined rather than disappearing, and the residue is the shape the repo already accepts (setup-dependencies.sh main, bump_versions.py main) -- a flag table plus a dispatcher. The reason to take it anyway is the clone: a change to the paired-sign-test tiering rule currently has to be made twice, and nothing fails if it is made once.
linux/llm-stack/benchmark_openai_api.py | benchmark_chat | 183 | Three jobs in one generator: the warmup request (464-480), one chat request in two transport flavours (503-548, of which 509-535 is the SSE reader), and ~75 lines of pure arithmetic that turn locals into the 20-key result dict (550-625: avg cpu/ram, the estimated-token fallback, tokens_per_sec, the LB2 ttft/decode_tps/prefill_tps split, the LB3 thinking_char_share). The seam is two extractions with no behaviour change — _consume_sse(response) -> (content, usage, streamed_chunks, first_token_at) out of 507-535, and _derive_result(i, prompt, content, usage, streamed_chunks, first_token_at, start, elapsed, before, after, busiest) -> dict out of 550-625 — leaving a ~50-line driver. What makes it worth doing rather than a paper win: the derivation is the part with the interesting logic and it is nearly untestable where it sits. tests/test_backend_compat.py::TestSseDialects drives the whole generator against a stub HTTP server and only covers the happy path with server-reported usage (it asserts tokens_estimated is False); the branch that matters — 572-576, where a server that omits the usage chunk gets its completion_tokens estimated from streamed_chunks — plus decode_tps at 588-591, prefill_tps at 592-593 and thinking_char_share at 600-603 are reachable today only through tests/test_harness_against_ollama.py, which is pytestmark-skipped whenever no live server answers. Outside the build closure; a pure function would be testable with no server at all.
linux/llm-stack/benchmark_openai_api.py | collect_hardware_info | 106 | Five independent probes, each wrapped in its own try so a missing source cannot lose the others — platform (58-66), /proc/cpuinfo (69-93), /proc/meminfo (96-105), /proc/1/cgroup (108-113), and the LB9 portable psutil/platform fallback (121-139) — closed by the incomplete list at 143-146. Nothing binds them together except the shared info dict, so the seam is mechanical: _os_facts(), _cpuinfo_facts(), _meminfo_facts(), _portable_facts() each returning a dict, merged by a ~12-line collector; that also removes the twice-walked first CPU paragraph, since 74-77 scans cpus[0] for "model name" and 87-90 scans the same paragraph again for "siblings", one pass over a {field: (key, cast)} table doing both. The reason to actually cut it rather than freeze it: nothing in tests/ names collect_hardware_info, and line 111 — info["in_container"] = "docker" in cgroup or "containerd" in cgroup or len(cgroup.splitlines()) > 0 — is true for any non-empty /proc/1/cgroup, so it reports in_container on every Linux host including a bare one; that is exactly the defect a per-source function with a fixture would have caught. And there is a better destination than a private helper: bench_provenance.collect() is the newer collector with the same explicit-incomplete convention and real tests (tests/test_bench_provenance.py), and the downstream consumers already treat the two blocks as interchangeable (bench_report.py:81 does doc.get("hardware") or doc.get("provenance"), bench_compare.py:130 files this file's hardware under "provenance"), so the CPU/RAM facts this one has and collect() lacks belong in collect().
linux/llm-stack/benchmark_openai_api.py | main | 170 | The argparse block (877-908) is 32 of these lines and is a TABLE — thirteen flags with long help strings and zero decisions — so it is not the problem; the problem is that four separate programs are inlined below it instead of dispatched. They are --list-backends (912-914, already a one-line call), --load (923-930, read a JSON and re-render it), --correctness-only (935-951, a whole alternate run with its own three-way exit ladder: 1 for unreachable, 1 for wrong, 2 for truncated, 0 for clean), and the actual benchmark (953-1045). The seam is _show_saved(path), _correctness_only(args, model, entry) and _run(args, model, entry) -> output-dict, leaving main as parse-then-dispatch. The payoff is coverage of the crash-safety path that only _run contains and nothing exercises: the .partial.jsonl side file written per result (973-975, 991-993), the KeyboardInterrupt arm (994-998), the atomic tmp-then-os.replace write plus partial cleanup (1036-1041) and the exit 130. That contract IS already tested for the sibling writer — tests/test_bench_cli.py::TestWriteReport::test_the_write_is_atomic against bench_cli.write_report — and cannot simply be delegated to it, because write_report imposes the benchmark/provenance/config/reports envelope while this file's readers (bench_report._ok_results, bench_compare.py:130) expect results/hardware; the reusable piece is only write_report's tmp+os.replace tail.
linux/llm-stack/benchmark_openai_api.py | print_table | 89 | Two programs under one name: 753-789 renders the per-prompt table (headers, col_widths, the fmt_row closure and one row per result with an ERROR arm), and 791-841 is a separate aggregate report — min/avg/max of latency, tok/s, CPU and RAM, the token totals, then the optional LB2 TTFT/decode/prefill block, the LB8 busiest-process rollup and the LB3 time-to-answer and thinking-share lines. The seam is print_rows(results) + print_summary(results). Two concrete reasons it is worth taking: the aggregation is a hand-rolled second copy of bench_report.summarise, which computes the same tokens_per_sec / answer_s / ttft_s / cpu_percent / ram_used_gb / completion_tokens over the same "error" not in r filter using _ok_results and _mean and is covered by tests/test_bench_report.py; and here that filter predicate is typed out six times at 791-796, once per metric list, where a single ok = [r for r in results if "error" not in r] (literally bench_report._ok_results) serves all of them. Nothing in tests/ names print_table, so today the whole summary — including the min() calls at 800-803 that would raise on an all-error run were they not guarded by the `if latencies` above them — is only ever exercised by a human reading a live run.
